---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'Hi-Vis Class 3 Apparel: When You Need Class 3 Over Class 2',
 description:
 'Learn which inputs your employer and project safety plan use to select Class 2 or Class 3 high-visibility apparel, and how decoration must be reviewed.',
 category: 'Compliance',
 readTime: '9 min read',
 date: 'May 9, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-05-09',
 image: '/images/generated/workwear-flatlay.jpg',
 slug: 'hi-vis-class-3-when-you-need-it-over-class-2',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'When do I need Class 3 instead of Class 2?', a: 'Your employer, hazard assessment, traffic-control plan, contract documents, and current governing requirements determine the required class. Do not select a class from a generic speed rule alone.' },
 { q: 'What is the difference between Class 2 and Class 3?', a: 'They are different performance classes under the current high-visibility apparel standard. Compare the exact product certificate and design with the requirements identified by your safety lead.' },
 { q: 'Can separate garments create an approved ensemble?', a: 'Do not assume separate garments form an approved ensemble. The employer must confirm the exact top, lower-body garment, labels, and current manufacturer documentation against its hazard assessment and project rules before use.' },
 { q: 'Does every highway job require the same class?', a: 'No universal website rule replaces the project traffic-control plan or contract specification. Confirm the requirement for the exact task, roadway, visibility, and jurisdiction.' },
 { q: 'Can I add a logo to Class 3 apparel?', a: 'Only when the exact garment manufacturer\'s current written instructions permit the proposed method, material, size, and placement, and the employer approves it against the current hazard assessment and project rules. Do not assume decoration preserves the garment\'s classification.' },
 { q: 'How do I verify a qualifying garment?', a: 'The employer should review the exact garment label and current manufacturer instructions and documentation against its hazard assessment and project requirements. Similar-looking fluorescent apparel may not carry the same classification.' },
 { q: 'When should high-visibility apparel be replaced?', a: 'Follow the exact manufacturer care and retirement instructions, plus your employer inspection program. Replace damaged, contaminated, altered, or degraded garments when the governing instructions require it.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 { '@type': 'Article', headline: post.title, description: post.description, image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`, datePublished: post.isoDate, dateModified: '2026-08-21', author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' } }, mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` },
 { '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` }] },
 { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) },
 ],
}

const speakableSchema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@type': 'WebPage',
 url: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 name: post.title,
 speakable: {
  '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
  cssSelector: ['h1', '.speakable-answer'],
 },
}
---

<BaseLayout title={post.title} description={post.description} canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`} ogImage={post.image} type="article" publishedDate={post.isoDate}>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(speakableSchema)} />
 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">
 <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-20 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 lg:px-8">
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Home</a><span class="px-2">/</span><a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Blog</a></nav>
 <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">{post.category}</span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">A planning guide to the documents and approvals that control Class 2 and Class 3 high-visibility apparel selection.</p>
 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash"><span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span>{post.readTime}</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time></div>
 </div>
 </header>
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8"><img src={post.image} alt="Representative high-visibility apparel shown for classification planning" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" /></div>
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Your employer's current hazard assessment and project safety plan determine whether a worker needs Class 2, Class 3, or another high-visibility configuration. The employer must confirm the exact product label, current manufacturer documentation, and any proposed decoration before use.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="the-rule" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">The ANSI 107 Rule on Class 3</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal"><a href="https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/isea/ansiisea1072020" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600" rel="noopener">ANSI/ISEA 107</a> defines performance classes for high-visibility safety apparel. The applicable edition, garment class, and task requirement must come from current official documents, the employer hazard assessment, and the project safety plan.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For the full Class 2 vs Class 3 background, see our <a href="/blog/ansi-107-class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">ANSI 107 Class 2 vs Class 3 guide for Las Vegas crews</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="when-class-3" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">When You Need Class 3 Specifically</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Review the worker's task, traffic exposure, visibility, equipment movement, contract documents, and jurisdiction. Treat the employer's written selection as the order requirement. Do not infer a garment class from a job title or road name.</p>

 <h2 id="osha-mutcd" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">OSHA, MUTCD, and the Class 3 Mandate</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Use current <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1926/1926.201" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600" rel="noopener">OSHA</a>, <a href="https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600" rel="noopener">MUTCD</a>, project, and agency requirements. Bighorn does not determine which standard or garment class applies to the work.</p>

 <h2 id="garment-spec" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">How to Confirm a Class 3 Garment</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The employer must use the exact product label and current manufacturer documentation to identify the garment's class. Do not classify a garment from a photo, sleeve length, color, or generic product name.</p>

 <h2 id="logo-rules" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Reviewing a Proposed Logo on Class 3 Apparel</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Decoration limits depend on the exact garment design and the manufacturer's current written instructions. The employer must approve the proposed method, material, size, and placement against its current hazard assessment and project rules. Do not assume a universal logo dimension applies or that decoration preserves classification.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For additional planning questions, read our <a href="/blog/custom-safety-vests-ansi-compliance/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">custom safety vest guide</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="vegas-context" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Class 3 in Vegas — Practical Reality</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Las Vegas heat adds another employer-controlled requirement. Evaluate garment classification, breathability, fit, care, heat controls, and the worker's task together. See our <a href="/custom-safety-vests-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">custom safety vest planning page</a> and <a href="/blog/nevada-osha-heat-rules-2026-workwear/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">Nevada heat-rule guide</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="related" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Related Reading</h2>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><a href="/blog/ansi-107-class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">ANSI 107 Class 2 vs Class 3 hi-vis</a></li>
 <li><a href="/blog/custom-safety-vests-ansi-compliance/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">Custom safety vests and ANSI 107 compliance</a></li>
 <li><a href="/blog/ppe-branded-workwear-regulations-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">PPE and branded workwear regulations</a></li>
 <li><a href="/industries/general-contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">General contractor crew apparel</a></li>
 </ul>

 <div class="mt-12 border border-hairline bg-gold-500/5 p-7 sm:p-9">
 <h2 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Plan decoration for employer-selected Class 3 apparel</h2>
<p class="mb-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Bighorn Threads coordinates a proposed decoration plan through production partners. Your employer selects the class from its current hazard assessment and project rules, then approves the exact garment and decoration using the manufacturer's current written instructions and documentation. Bighorn does not certify the garment or determine workplace safety compliance.</p>
 <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="inline-flex min-h-[48px] items-center gap-2 bg-gold-500 px-7 py-3 text-base font-bold text-charcoal transition-all hover:bg-gold-600">Get a Quote →</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </article>
</BaseLayout>
